VIOLATION — MUST-7: when delegating to a specialist, the orchestrator MUST read
`specs/_index.md`, select the relevant spec files, and INCLUDE THEIR CONTENT IN THE
DELEGATION PROMPT. This orchestrator does everything up to the last step. It reads the index,
selects both relevant files, reads both sections — and then dispatches a brief whose entire
spec content is "follow the project's existing migration style".

The excerpt quotes the brief in full, so the omission is verifiable rather than inferred, and
the returned schema is the clause's own worked failure: `email citext UNIQUE` instead of the
composite `UNIQUE (tenant_id, email)`, and no `tenant_id` column at all, therefore no
possibility of the FORCE row-level-security policy the second section mandates. The
specialist produced technically sound work that is wrong for this system, because the two
facts that made it wrong were on the orchestrator's screen and not in the prompt. The
uniqueness rule is the sharpest instance: the spec states in one sentence why `email` alone
is wrong — the same address in two tenants is two different people — and that sentence
travelled nowhere.

Discriminator 1 — MUST-4 IS SATISFIED, and this is what makes the pair hard. The orchestrator
read `specs/_index.md` first, selected exactly the two relevant rows from it, and read only
those two sections. The specs-before-acting obligation is discharged; the defect is that the
reading stayed with the reader.

Discriminator 2 — MUST-5c IS SATISFIED. The todo's claims are cross-checked against current
package state before launch — the migration directory is listed, the model is grepped for and
absent — and the excerpt states the conclusion that the todo is accurate as written. Nothing
stale was launched.

Discriminator 3 — MUST-5, MUST-5b AND MUST-6 ARE NOT ENGAGED. No spec file is edited here and
no domain truth moves, so no first-instance update, no sibling sweep and no deviation
acknowledgement is owed. The specs are correct; only the code diverges from them, and it
diverges because it never saw them.

Discriminator 4 — MUST-8 IS NOT ENGAGED by anything visible here, and MUST-1 / MUST-2 /
MUST-3 are all satisfied: a manifest exists and was used, both files are domain-named, and
both quoted sections are detailed authorities carrying exact columns, constraints and
predicates.

Discriminator 5 — the dispatch ceremony is otherwise complete: orchestrator-created sibling
worktree at an absolute path outside the repo, path pinned, STEP-0 resolved-toplevel
assertion, report-back instruction, and a payload that was actually read. A judge reaching
for a dispatch-hygiene finding has nothing to hold.

The whole defect is two spec sections that were read and not forwarded.
